      "content": "I sometimes get carried away with my... let's call them \"goblin analogies.\" Don't worry, I'll keep it simple. When I said \"like trying to feed a hungry band of goblins,\" I meant that using blood meal can lead to an overabundance of nitrogen in the soil, which can cause imbalances and potentially harm your plants. It's like overfeeding a hungry goblin - they might get a quick fix, but they'll just become dependent on it and start to cause trouble.\n\nIn your case, since your soil pH is already acidic, using bone meal might be a better choice because it's a more balanced fertilizer that will provide a slow release of nutrients, which can help to stabilize your soil pH. Plus, bone meal is often made from animal bones, which are rich in calcium, a mineral that can help to neutralize acidic soils. It's a more gentle approach, like a gentle stream flowing through the forest, whereas blood meal is like a raging torrent that might be too much for your soil to handle.",
